{"tool_name":"phencards","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"phencards","home":"https://phencards.org/","summary":"PhenCards is a web server for linking human phenotype information to biomedical knowledge.","addition_date":"2021-11-01T14:09:29.442854Z","last_update_date":"2021-11-01T14:12:04.037728Z","tool_type":["Web application"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:phencards","credit":[{"email":"wangk@email.chop.edu","name":"Kai Wang","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5585-982X","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Query and retrieval","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0224"}]}],"name":"PhenCards","owner":"Kigaard","publication":[{"doi":"10.1186/S13073-021-00909-8","metadata":{"abstract":"\u00a9 2021, The Author(s).We present PhenCards (https://phencards.org), a database and web server intended as a one-stop shop for previously disconnected biomedical knowledge related to human clinical phenotypes. Users can query human phenotype terms or clinical notes. PhenCards obtains relevant disease/phenotype prevalence and co-occurrence, drug, procedural, pathway, literature, grant, and collaborator data. PhenCards recommends the most probable genetic diseases and candidate genes based on phenotype terms from clinical notes. PhenCards facilitates exploration of phenotype, e.g., which drugs cause or are prescribed for patient symptoms, which genes likely cause specific symptoms, and which comorbidities co-occur with phenotypes.","authors":[{"name":"Dong X."},{"name":"Havrilla J.M."},{"name":"Liu C."},{"name":"Wang K."},{"name":"Weng C."}],"citationCount":1,"date":"2021-12-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Genome Medicine","title":"PhenCards: a data resource linking human phenotype information to biomedical knowledge"},"pmcid":"PMC8147460","pmid":"34034817","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Genotype and phenotype","uri":"http://edamontology.org